nswer":1,"solution":"<p>The given reaction is for preparation of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>DDT</mi></math> (dichlorodiphenyl trichloroethane). Complete reaction is given as follows.</p><p>(i)</p><p><img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fbitsat%252Fc49419d1-5434-42c7-b515-e22645f3c73a-image.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fbitsat%2Fc49419d1-5434-42c7-b515-e22645f3c73a-image.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fbitsat%2Fc49419d1-5434-42c7-b515-e22645f3c73a-image.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 300px; height: 130px;\" /></p><p>(ii) <img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fbitsat%252F230d6a1e-e8e5-4e0d-9c43-c0793d7fc9ca-image.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fbitsat%2F230d6a1e-e8e5-4e0d-9c43-c0793d7fc9ca-image.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fbitsat%2F230d6a1e-e8e5-4e0d-9c43-c0793d7fc9ca-image.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 500px; height: 97px;\" /></p><p>Dichloro-diphenyl-trichloroethane (DDT) is prepared by heating chloral and chlorobenzene in 1:2 ratio in the presence of conc. sulphuric acid.</p><p>DDT is used as an insecticide. It is also used as a mosquito repellant.</p>","difficulty":"Hard","source":"BITSAT 2019","_marksId":"64c0e8086f5bfc7ab78ae9fb","answers":[1],"questionType":"singleCorrect"},{"id":17625,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Haloalkanes and Haloarenes","exam":"Engineering","examName":"BITSAT","q":"<p>Identify compound <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></mfenced></math> in the following reaction</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>8</mn></msub><mover><mo>→</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>Cl</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi></mrow></mover><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></mfenced><mover><mo>→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Br</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>Fe</mi></mrow></mover><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></mfenced><mover><mo>→</mo><mrow><mi>Zn</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>HCl</mi></mrow></mover><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></mfenced></math></p>","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi></math>-bromotoluene","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math>-bromotoluene","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi></math>-bromotoluene","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn></math>-bromo, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo> </mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mo> </mo><mn>6</mn></math> trichlorotoluene"],"answer":1,"solution":"<p>Side chain hydrogen atoms are substituted by halogens in presence of light or heat by free radical mechanism. Ring hydrogens are substituted in presence of Lewis acid by electrofile. Alkyl halide (R-X) are reduced by Zn/HCl into alkane.</p><p>The degree of unsaturation for compound <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>8</mn></msub></math> is three <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mn>3</mn></mfenced></math>, as it reacts with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>Cl</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mo>.</mo></math> Hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>8</mn></msub></math> is toluene, i.e <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>.</mo><msub><mi>CH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>.</mo></math> The whole reaction occurs as follows:</p><p><img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fbitsat%252Fb5ccc3ad-260d-4c39-98bf-fbb659318bee-image.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fbitsat%2Fb5ccc3ad-260d-4c39-98bf-fbb659318bee-image.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fbitsat%2Fb5ccc3ad-260d-4c39-98bf-fbb659318bee-image.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 742px; height: 198px;\" /></p>\n<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fbitsat%2Fb5ccc3ad-260d-4c39-98bf-fbb659318bee-image.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 742px; height: 198px;\" />","difficulty":"Medium","source":"BITSAT 2019","_marksId":"64c0e8096f5bfc7ab78aea0b"},{"id":17626,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Haloalkanes and Haloarenes","exam":"Engineering","examName":"BITSAT","q":"<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Chlorobenzene</mi><mover><mo>→</mo><mrow><mi>Reaction</mi><mo>-</mo><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">X</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mover><mi>Phenol</mi><mover><mo>→</mo><mrow><mi>Reaction</mi><mo>-</mo><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Y</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mover><mi>Salicylaldehyde</mi></math>.</p><p>The reactions(s) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mmultiscripts><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">X</mi><none></none><mo>'</mo><mprescripts></mprescripts><none></none><mo>'</mo></mmultiscripts></math> and<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mmultiscripts><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Y</mi><none></none><mo>'</mo><mprescripts></mprescripts><none></none><mo>'</mo></mmultiscripts></math> respectively are</p>","options":["Fries rearrangement and Kolbe","Cumene and Reimer-Tiemann","Dow and Reimer-Tiemann","Dow and Sandmeyer."],"answer":2,"solution":"<p>Phenol is manufactured by heating chlorobenzene with 10% aqueous NaOH solution at about 623K under 300 atm and in the presence of copper salt acting as a catalyst to form sodium phenoxide. The sodium salt when treated with dil. HCl, gives phenol. This method is known as Dow's process. </p><p>When phenol is heated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>CHCl</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math> in the presence of aqueous NaOH, an aldehydic group (−CHO) get introduced in the ring at a position ortho to the phenolic group. This reaction is known as the Reimer-Tiemann reaction.</p><p><img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fbitsat%252F6d6bb09a-1ad7-45bc-911a-55f8587d5688-image.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fbitsat%2F6d6bb09a-1ad7-45bc-911a-55f8587d5688-image.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fbitsat%2F6d6bb09a-1ad7-45bc-911a-55f8587d5688-image.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 